{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-006",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "C# classes structs records constructors and initialization",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A type declaration defines a reusable contract and representation. Classes are reference types; structs and enums are value types. Records add value-oriented equality and nondestructive mutation syntax without automatically making referenced data deeply immutable.",
    "Constructors establish valid state. Instance constructors, static constructors, primary constructors, object initializers, required members, and field/property initializers have distinct execution and initialization rules.",
    "A class instance has identity and can participate in inheritance. A struct is copied by value and cannot inherit from another user-defined class or struct, though it can implement interfaces; ref structs add restrictions to prevent unsafe lifetime escape.",
    "Records provide concise data-oriented declarations: record class is a reference type, record struct a value type. Positional records synthesize members, but equality and with-copy behavior are shallow with respect to referenced members.",
    "C# 15 introduces union declarations and closed hierarchies. A union represents one of named cases, while a closed class limits direct derivation to its assembly; these features complement, rather than replace, class/struct/record design."
  ],
